    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Tour for Igor line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот, Игорь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here you go, Igor.' — Built around the key proper name meaning 'Igor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это оазис французской кухни.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is an oasis of French cuisine.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'oasis' directly with 'French', combining 'oasis' with 'French' and 'kitchen'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Анклав качества.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A bastion of quality.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'enclave' directly with 'quality', combining 'enclave' with 'quality'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Сколько сил вложено сюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So much effort went into this place.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'how much' directly with 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Официант, как родной ребенок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A waiter is like my own child.' The question word for 'how' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'child'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "О, привет, Виляш.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, hi, Vilyash.' Nagiyev delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'hi'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я Настя, вообще-то.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am Nastya, actually.' Nastya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'actually'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Новенькая, наверное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She must be new.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'new girl' directly with 'probably', combining 'new girl' with 'probably'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну, пойдем дальше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, let's keep going.' Nagiyev chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'let's go' for the ongoing action, followed by 'next'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Сделай нам два кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Make us two coffees.' Dmitry uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'make'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это Виктория Сергеевна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is Viktoria Sergeyevna.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'this' directly with 'Viktoriya', combining 'this' with 'Viktoriya' and 'Sergeyevna'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Арт-директор нашего ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The art director of our restaurant.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'art director' straight to 'restaurant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
